## The short answer, by repair method

A slab leak is not one job with one price. It is a diagnosis followed by a choice, and the choice drives the
cost far more than the leak itself does.

| Stage or method | Typical San Diego County range | What it covers |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Electronic leak detection | **[DETECTION PRICE RANGE]** | Pressure testing, acoustic listening, line tracing, and a marked location |
| Spot repair through the slab | **[SPOT REPAIR RANGE]** | Opening the slab at one point, repairing the pipe, patching the concrete |
| Overhead reroute | **[REROUTE RANGE]** | Abandoning the failed line and running new pipe through walls and attic |
| Whole-house repipe | **[REPIPE RANGE]** | Replacing the supply system in PEX or copper, with patched access openings |

Detection comes first in every case, and it is money well spent: without it, a repair crew is guessing at the
location of a pipe encased in concrete, and demolition costs scale with the size of the guess.

## Why East County is slab leak territory

Slab leaks are not evenly distributed across the county. They cluster in housing built during specific eras,
and East County has a great deal of it.

**El Cajon** — Much of the city went up between the 1950s and 1970s on slab foundations, with copper supply
lines routed beneath the concrete. Fletcher Hills, Bostonia, and Granite Hills produce a steady stream of these
calls. Fifty-plus years of hard water and soil movement is simply a long time for soft copper.

**Santee** — The 1970s and 1980s tracts through Carlton Hills and around Santee Lakes were built quickly, on
slabs, with the same copper-under-concrete approach. Because whole neighbourhoods went up within a few years of
each other, they tend to start failing on a similar schedule.

**Rancho San Diego** — The master-planned communities around Jamacha Valley feel newer, but homes from the
1980s and 1990s are now 25 to 40 years old, and the thin-wall copper of that building boom is prone to pinhole
failures from the inside out.

Add sustained high water pressure — routine on East County's hillside lots — and copper fatigues faster than
its designers ever intended.

## Warning signs worth acting on

Slab leaks rarely produce a puddle. They produce oddities:

- A warm or hot patch on a tile or vinyl floor, which usually means the hot-water line
- The faint sound of running water when every fixture in the house is off
- A Helix or Padre Dam bill that jumps with no change in household habits
- Hairline cracks appearing in flooring, or moisture at the edge of the slab
- Damp carpet edges, a musty smell, or baseboards that feel soft
- A water heater that seems to run constantly

Any one of those justifies a look. Two together make it urgent.

## How to run a meter test yourself

Drought-conscious San Diego homeowners often get their first warning from a water district usage alert. You can
confirm it in about fifteen minutes without any tools:

1. Turn off every fixture and water-using appliance in the house — taps, ice maker, irrigation, everything.
2. Find your water meter at the street and look for the small triangular or gear-shaped **leak indicator**.
3. Watch it for a minute or two. If it is turning at all, water is moving somewhere with nothing turned on.
4. Note the meter reading, wait an hour without using water, and read it again. Any change confirms a leak.

A moving indicator does not prove the leak is under the slab — it could be irrigation, a toilet, or a yard line.
It does prove that something needs finding, which is what detection equipment is for.

## What moves the price up or down

Four factors account for most of the variation between quotes:

**Where the leak is.** A leak near the edge of the slab, in a garage, or under a closet is far cheaper to reach
than one under a kitchen island or a load-bearing wall.

**Hot line versus cold line.** Hot-water lines fail more often and are usually easier to confirm, but they
frequently run through the middle of the house rather than around its perimeter.

**Flooring type.** This is the surprise item on many invoices. Opening a slab under vinyl or carpet is
straightforward. Doing it under tile, stone, or finished hardwood means matching or replacing material that may
be discontinued — and that restoration is often the largest line on the bill.

**Whether a reroute makes more sense.** In a single-storey home, running a new line overhead frequently costs
less than cutting concrete and restoring the floor, and it retires the vulnerable pipe permanently instead of
patching one point on it.

## What insurance usually covers

Homeowners policies in California commonly cover two things a slab leak causes: the cost of **accessing** the
pipe, and the **water damage** that resulted. What they typically exclude is the failed pipe itself, on the
grounds that it wore out rather than being damaged by a covered event.

That distinction is why documentation matters. We provide a written detection report, photographs, and an
itemised invoice that separates detection, access, plumbing repair, and restoration — the breakdown adjusters
ask for. We are also happy to speak with your adjuster directly.

## One leak or a pattern?

The honest question after a first slab leak is whether it is an isolated failure or the opening act.

One leak in an otherwise sound system deserves a spot repair or a reroute. Two or three within a couple of
years usually means the pipe walls are failing generally, and each "cheap" fix buys less time than the last. At
that point a [water line repair and repiping](/services/water-lines/) quote is worth having in hand, because a
repipe ends the cycle rather than extending it.

We will show you the pipe sections we remove so that decision rests on evidence rather than a sales pitch. For
detection and repair anywhere in East County, see
[slab leak detection and repair in El Cajon](/services/slab-leak-repair/), or
[slab leak repair in Santee](/services/slab-leak-repair/santee/) if that is closer to home.
